1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Hello, this is Yamyam Coding.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:08,500
What you are looking at is the front page of
the Steam Next Fest running today. Every

3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:12,580
blurred tile is a demo whose developer wrote
down that generative AI was used. Three years

4
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:17,720
ago this event had a little over a thousand
demos. Now it has more than eight thousand.

5
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:22,380
I think this screen is where the future of AI
game development arrived first.

6
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,640
Today we will briefly check what happened at
Next Fest, then look at how those

7
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:31,140
numbers changed the people who make games,
what players actually got out of it,

8
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:34,520
what happened to the indie market that used to
compete on ideas,

9
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:39,200
and what a developer preparing for the next
three years should do.

10
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:41,260
First, what happened.

11
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:45,000
Next Fest is the event that gathers demos of
unreleased games,

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,760
three times a year. In 2022 and 2023 it had
around a thousand demos;

13
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:54,920
in 2025 about two thousand six hundred, this
June about four thousand four hundred,

14
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:58,480
and this October, by Valve's count, more than
eight thousand two hundred.

15
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:02,100
Trackers differ a little, but that is close to
an eightfold rise in three years.

16
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:06,000
Across all of Steam, twenty thousand games
were released last year alone,

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,700
and half of them have fewer than ten reviews.

18
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:13,400
And the day after this fest opened, a Reddit
post saying that AI had

19
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:17,420
absolutely ruined Next Fest passed eight
thousand eight hundred upvotes.

20
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:21,520
Finding a decent demo, it said, was harder
than winning the lottery.

21
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:26,240
Counted properly, more than 36 percent of this
fest's demos disclose generative AI.

22
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:30,340
Four months ago it was around 20 percent.

23
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:36,160
These numbers say one thing: making got
cheaper, and choosing got expensive.

24
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:41,340
But first we need to be precise about what 36
percent used AI actually means.

25
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:46,740
Steam's AI disclosure changed three times in
three years. In June 2023 Valve refused to

26
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:53,660
release a game containing AI art, because the
rights to the training material were unclear.

27
00:01:54,420 --> 00:02:01,260
In January 2024 it became allowed as long as
it was written down. Content made

28
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:06,060
during development, Pre-Generated, and content
made while the game runs,

29
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:13,520
Live-Generated, go into the survey, and that
text appears at the very

30
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,380
bottom of the store page in a box called the
AI Generated Content Disclosure.

31
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:20,620
Then early this year the scope narrowed: only
content

32
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:25,080
that ships with the game and that players see
and hear.

33
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:28,980
Efficiency gains from development tools are
outside the section's focus.

34
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:32,780
So using an AI assistant while writing code is
not disclosed,

35
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:36,320
while making the capsule art or background
music with AI is.

36
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:40,020
I think this line is the line that will divide
game development from here on.

37
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:45,600
Using AI as a tool and putting AI's output on
screen are two different things.

38
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,200
This year GDC asked two thousand three hundred
developers, and 36 percent said they

39
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:55,780
use generative AI in their work. The uses:
research and brainstorming 81 percent,

40
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:00,000
code assistance 47 percent, prototyping 35
percent. Mostly tools.

41
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:02,680
But what is used as a tool leaks onto the
screen.

42
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:07,280
A major title released this March shipped with
a few 2D props made

43
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,600
experimentally during early iteration, and
players found them within hours.

44
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:15,540
The developer apologized and promised to audit
every asset and replace them.

45
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:19,380
Among this fest's disclosures, many games
wrote placeholder images.

46
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:23,060
A temporary asset that stays to the end: that
is the most common accident right now.

47
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:27,420
So how is the making side changing? Three
things are visible.

48
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:31,960
First, solo developers and small teams make
more.

49
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:36,540
Eight times the demos, twenty thousand
releases a year.

50
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:41,220
A reporter who read the disclosures at Next
Fest found small teams and solo developers

51
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,360
citing limited resources as the reason. Making
is no longer the bottleneck.

52
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:46,480
Second, the makers' feelings are going the
other way. In the same GDC survey,

53
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:52,360
52 percent said generative AI is having a
negative effect on the industry:

54
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:59,600
18 percent two years ago, 30 percent last
year, 52 percent this year.

55
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:04,680
Only 7 percent said positive. Visual artists
at 64 percent and designers and narrative

56
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:08,160
staff at 63 percent are the most negative.
More people use it, fewer people like it.

57
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,140
Third, studios have started drawing lines. The
publisher of Manor Lords has written

58
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:17,680
an AI-asset ban into its contracts since
January. The reason is precise: it only

59
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:23,500
takes one placeholder from a prototype
slipping into a build without being replaced.

60
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,840
The developer of Warframe stated that nothing
in its games will ever be AI-generated,

61
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:31,140
and as of this July there is a published list
of fifty-nine

62
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:34,300
studios that have declared they do not use
generative AI.

63
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:38,380
So over the next three years the competition
moves from

64
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:41,240
whether you can make it to what you can prove
a human made.

65
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:43,800
So what did players get out of this? Eight
times the choice,

66
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:47,980
but not better games at a lower price.

67
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:52,540
Quality first. The Next Fest hub is shuffled
at random for the first few days,

68
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:57,180
the disclosure sits at the very bottom of the
page, and the first impression is a single

69
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:02,360
small capsule image. One publisher's lead said
an AI-made capsule is an immediate turn-off.

70
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:07,840
One games outlet wrote that this fest's
worthless vibe-coded slop had leveled up.

71
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:13,120
So players built their own sieve. The blurred
tiles in the opening shot are exactly that.

72
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:18,120
An open-source extension called AI warning for
Steam shows the bottom-of-page disclosure

73
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:23,920
as a pop-up and blurs or hides disclosed games
in search results. No-AI curators appeared,

74
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:29,020
and itch.io has required AI-generated and No
AI tags since late 2024. There is an opposing

75
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:35,440
view: the head of Epic Games called this kind
of disclosure a scarlet letter pinned on

76
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:39,580
developers. Either way, the work of choosing
has moved from the platform to the player.

77
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:44,180
Next, price. If making got cheaper, prices
should have fallen.

78
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:47,780
They did not. Seventy dollars became the
default for big releases,

79
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:50,720
and last year Nintendo shipped an
eighty-dollar game.

80
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:55,300
The average price of Steam's top fifty new
releases went from 21.8 dollars in

81
00:05:55,300 --> 00:06:00,140
early 2023 to 21.4 dollars in autumn 2025:
almost flat over two and a half years.

82
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:04,480
The median fell from 19.5 to 15.6 dollars, but
that is because many

83
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:10,000
five-to-fifteen-dollar games sold well, not
because the same games got cheaper.

84
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:12,860
If anything, the cost of making games properly
went up. The estimated development

85
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:18,020
budget behind 2025's new Steam releases set a
record at 27 billion dollars, and the

86
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:23,120
average indie budget is around a hundred
thousand dollars, up 156 percent in ten years.

87
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:26,620
In short, players are getting more games, in
more noise,

88
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:30,960
at the same price. I think this is the picture
of the quality loss and

89
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:34,220
the price-to-quality burden of AI use being
passed on to players.

90
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:39,640
And the indie market, which used to compete on
ideas, got harder. The reason is cloning.

91
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:45,960
This August a small studio's Needle In A
Haystack Simulator went viral on social media.

92
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:49,880
Similarly titled games started appearing on
Steam overnight,

93
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:52,820
and one reporter counted at least fourteen
clones.

94
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:55,320
The clones even copied each other. One team
said it found thirteen of its classes

95
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:59,760
inside another team's game with identical
names, and an error message copied

96
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:05,160
word for word that points to a shader which
does not even exist in that game.

97
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:08,840
The reporter read this as a sign of code
decompiled with AI and copied.

98
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,720
The co-op climbing game Peak saw
near-identical knockoffs

99
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:14,420
appear on a console store within weeks of
going viral,

100
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:18,400
and on Roblox one creator cloned another game
within twenty-four hours

101
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:22,460
from a single video and said the AI did
everything while they sat and watched.

102
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:25,380
Korean press covered the same story this week.
One Japanese developer received an extortion

103
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:29,800
email claiming its game had been cloned with
AI, with no proof attached, and another cut

104
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:34,760
back its development updates to avoid being
copied. The developer that cancelled its

105
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:41,040
public demo three days before this fest and
switched to a closed lottery test gave exactly

106
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:45,660
this reason: once gameplay spreads, AI-made
imitations can ship before the real game.

107
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:50,860
In numbers, indie revenue was already a narrow
door. Of the twenty thousand games released

108
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:56,960
on Steam in 2025, 608, three percent, passed a
thousand reviews. Half have fewer than ten.

109
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:01,700
When clones line up at that door first, the
time the original has to sell gets shorter.

110
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:05,880
One market analyst put it this way: good games
will get cloned and buried,

111
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,820
and players will default to the franchises
they know.

112
00:08:09,080 --> 00:08:12,740
Because an idea gets copied the moment you
show it, indies have to hide it;

113
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:16,600
if they hide it they cannot promote it; and if
they cannot promote it,

114
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:20,300
it does not sell. Over the next three years an
idea on its own is no longer a moat.

115
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:25,240
So two processes appear inside studios: having
people re-check every asset

116
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:30,360
before release and record where AI was used,
and deciding what to show and when.

117
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:35,940
To sum up: in three years AI has nearly
removed the wall on the making side of games.

118
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:40,420
In exchange, players get more games at the
same price in more noise, and indies pass

119
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:44,700
through a narrower door in a market where
ideas must be hidden. The cost the makers

120
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:49,960
saved is being passed on to the players who
choose and the small studios who build.

121
00:08:50,420 --> 00:08:52,880
To be a star in a sky that went from a
thousand

122
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:54,840
demos to eight thousand, you need three
things.

123
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:59,040
First, use AI as a tool, but let humans own
what appears on screen and disclose honestly.

124
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:02,980
Track every placeholder to the end.

125
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:08,240
Second, spend time where the human hand shows:
the capsule, the art, the first ten seconds.

126
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:14,440
Third, make execution and people your moat,
not the idea. Community,

127
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:18,040
update speed and the trust of these people
made this game cannot be cloned in a day.

128
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:20,860
An eightfold wider sky cannot be undone.

129
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:24,120
Players are already building the sieve that
picks the stars,

130
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,800
and the next three years are the time to make
the games that pass through it.
